  "body": "### What is the issue with the HTML Standard?\n\n`PromiseRejectionError` constructor behaves differently depending on the implementation. Executing the following code:\n\n```js\nnew PromiseRejectionEvent(\"foo\", { promise: 1 })\n```\n\nAccording to the specification, [the `promise` property of `PromiseRejectionErrorInit` only accepts an `object`](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/webappapis.html#promiserejectioneventinit), so it seems that throwing a `TypeError` is correct. However, only Firefox implements this correctly. Chrome and Safari do not throw an exception and instead own `Promise.resolve(1)` as the `promise` property.\n\nFirefox:\n\n<img width=\"823\" height=\"61\" alt=\"Firefox throws a TypeError\" src=\"https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/428db269-2821-4119-ae5d-8bfbbeb31986\" />\n\nChrome:\n\n<img width=\"626\" height=\"308\" alt=\"Chrome does not throw a exception\" src=\"https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/4fe3820c-815e-4e76-b4a4-25b2962af57b\" />\n\nSafari:\n\n<img width=\"386\" height=\"307\" alt=\"Safari does not throw a exception\" src=\"https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/502b838d-88fc-4197-905d-b4955a2d1336\" />",
